WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.500
Lazy fetching defers data access until needed, while eager fetching requests related

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:06.750
data as part of loading policy.

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:10.449
Eager does not guarantee one SQL join or good performance.

00:00:10.599 --> 00:00:14.535
The N plus one pattern occurs when one query loads parents and

00:00:14.535 --> 00:00:17.158
subsequent accesses issue one additional query per parent.

00:00:17.308 --> 00:00:21.109
Measure query count and row volume rather than guessing from annotations.

00:00:21.259 --> 00:00:25.941
Choose a fetch plan for each use case: a DTO projection, entity

00:00:25.941 --> 00:00:28.282
graph, fetch join, or batch fetching.

00:00:28.432 --> 00:00:31.711
Joining several collections can multiply rows dramatically.

00:00:31.861 --> 00:00:36.281
Combining collection fetch joins with pagination can be unsafe or inefficient depending

00:00:36.281 --> 00:00:38.491
on the query and provider behavior.

00:00:38.641 --> 00:00:42.819
Keep the persistence boundary explicit with Open Session in View disabled.

00:00:42.969 --> 00:00:47.409
The product list maps simple product fields to DTOs, so it has

00:00:47.409 --> 00:00:48.519
no relationship traversal.

00:00:48.669 --> 00:00:52.847
After adding order lines, a dashboard that loads orders and then accesses

00:00:52.847 --> 00:00:55.284
each line collection can trigger many queries.

00:00:55.434 --> 00:00:59.407
A two-step paged query or projection can fetch only the data required

00:00:59.407 --> 00:01:00.400
by the dashboard.

00:01:00.550 --> 00:01:03.079
Create fifty orders with several lines each.

00:01:03.229 --> 00:01:07.833
Record SQL count for a lazy traversal, a DTO projection, and a

00:01:07.833 --> 00:01:08.984
bounded batch approach.

00:01:09.134 --> 00:01:12.547
Compare transferred rows and latency as well as query count.

00:01:12.697 --> 00:01:16.787
One enormous join is not automatically better than several bounded queries.

00:01:16.937 --> 00:01:21.777
Prevent accidental lazy access after the transaction ends by assembling the DTO

00:01:21.777 --> 00:01:23.390
inside the intended boundary.

00:01:23.540 --> 00:01:27.829
Verify the chosen plan on the target database and realistic data size.

00:01:27.979 --> 00:01:28.650
Question.

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:30.594
Does EAGER solve N plus one?

00:01:30.744 --> 00:01:31.321
Answer.

00:01:31.471 --> 00:01:32.465
Not reliably.

00:01:32.615 --> 00:01:37.140
It specifies when data must be available, not an optimal SQL strategy.

00:01:37.290 --> 00:01:37.961
Question.

00:01:38.111 --> 00:01:40.180
Why disable Open Session in View?

00:01:40.330 --> 00:01:40.907
Answer.

00:01:41.057 --> 00:01:46.605
It makes database access boundaries explicit and reduces unexpected queries during response

00:01:46.605 --> 00:01:47.068
rendering.
